\h Galatians
\toc1 Galatians
\toc2 Galatians
\toc3 Gal.
\mt2 Paulos’s letter to the assemblies in
\mt1 Galatia
\is1 Introduction
\ip At the time when the preaching of the good message about Yeshua began to spread and it started being accepted by non-Jews, an important question arose: whether or not it’s necessary for a person wanting to accept the message to also obey the Law of Mosheh. Paulos argues that it’s not necessary because it’s by faith that we can receive life from the messiah, and it’s by faith that a person is considered guiltless in God’s eyes. But there among the groups of believers in Galasya (a province of Rome in Asia Minor, now part of modern Turkey), there were people who disagreed with Paulos and believed that it’s still necessary for a follower of Yeshua to obey all of Mosheh’s laws in order to become righteous in God’s sight.
\ip In \bk Paulos’s letter to the assemblies in Galatia\bk*, he wrote so that people who had started to follow deviant teachings might return to the true faith. He began this by explaining the reasons that he himself became a missionary. He showed that his becoming a missionary wasn’t because he was selected by the people, but that God himself spoke to him, and what’s more, he was specifically sent by God to the non-Jews. As a result of that, he strove to ensure that people understood that there’s only one way to restore our relationship with God, and that’s through faith in God’s son.
\ip Nearer the end of his letter, he answered those who say that it’s wrong not to obey Mosheh’s law. Paulos stated that he would be afraid to leave behind God’s goodness and mercy just to become a slave of Mosheh’s rules. Again he advised that it’s necessary for them to listen to God’s spirit so that they’re changed and don’t follow the sinful desires of their own bodies.
\iot Main components of Paulos’s letter
\io1 Introduction \ior 1:1-10\ior*
\io1 Paulos’s calling as an ambassador of the messiah \ior 1:11–2:14\ior*
\io1 The good message about the goodness and mercy of God \ior 2:15–4:31\ior*
\io1 Christian freedom and living \ior 5:1–6:10\ior*
\io1 Ending \ior 6:11-18\ior*
\rem This is still an early look into the unpolished text of the Open English Translation of the Bible. Please double-check the text in advance before using in public.
\rem NOTE: This USFM file was exported from the ESFM source file with some loss of features, so don’t edit this derived file.
\c 1
\p
\v 1 \add This letter is from\add* Paulos, \nd one\nd* \add of Yeshua’s\add* \add (Jesus; Grk. Yesus)\add* \add ambassadors\add*, not chosen by people or any human desire, but \nd chosen\nd* \add directly\add* by \nd Yeshua the messiah\nd* and \nd God\nd* the father who raised \add Yeshua\add* from the dead.
\v 2 \add On behalf of\add* all the believers here, \add I’m writing\add* to the assemblies \add of believers\add* in Galatia.
\p
\v 3 May you all experience the grace and peace that comes from \nd God\nd* the father and our \nd master Yeshua the messiah\nd*,
\v 4 the one who gave himself for our sins to rescue us from this present, evil age, according to \nd God\nd* our father’s desire—
\v 5 \add our father\add* who’ll be praised even through future ages. That’s how it is.
\s1 There’s only one good message
\p
\v 6 I’m astounded that you \add people\add* have so quickly replaced the good message \add that you heard\add* from the \nd one\nd* who called you to the \nd messiah’s\nd* grace. You’ve replaced it with another narrative
\v 7 which is not at all another good message, but rather comes from those who are confusing you and wanting to alter the \add actual\add* good message about the \nd messiah\nd*.
\v 8 Even if either we ourselves or \add even\add* a messenger from heaven were to preach a different ‘good message’ which disagrees with the good message that we \add first\add* preached to you, then they should be cursed.
\v 9 As we’ve previously said, and even now say it again: if anyone preaches a ‘good message’ to you that’s different from what you already heard, let that person be cursed.
\p
\v 10 Am I trying to please and persuade people or \nd God\nd*? If I was still trying to please people, I wouldn’t be \add worthy to be\add* \nd one of the messiah’s\nd* \add slaves\add*.
\s1 Paulos was commissioned directly by God
\p
\v 11 I’m letting you know, \add brothers and sisters\add*, that the good message I proclaimed didn’t just come from human ideas.
\v 12 It didn’t come from my ideas, nor was I taught it by some other person, but it \add was revealed\add* to me by \nd Yeshua \+add (Jesus; Grk. Yesus)\+add* the messiah\nd*.
\p
\v 13 \add Brothers and sisters,\add* you’ve heard \nd about\nd* my previous behaviour, when as a strict Yehudi \add (Jew)\add*, I was extreme in persecuting the assembly \nd of God’s\nd* people, and was trying to destroy them.\x + \xo 1:13: \xt Acts 8:3; 22:4-5; 26:9-11.\x*
\v 14 I was progressing in Youdaismos quicker than many of my contemporaries, being so much more zealous in observing our ancient traditions.\x + \xo 1:14: \xt Acts 22:3.\x*
\p
\v 15 But \add God\add*, who’d planned this from even before I was born, called me,\x + \xo 1:15-16: \xt Acts 9:3-6; 22:6-10; 26:13-18.\x* because of his grace,
\v 16 so that he could reveal his son through me, and I could preach the good message to non-Jews. I didn’t rush to get advice from others,
\v 17 nor did I head to Yerushalem \add (Jerusalem)\add* to \add consult with\add* the ambassadors there that preceded me, but I went away to Arav and then later returned to Damesek \add (Damascus)\add*.
\v 18 Then \add finally\add*, after three years, I went to Yerushalem to get to know Kefa \add (Peter; Grk. Petros)\add*, and stayed with him for just over two weeks.\x + \xo 1:18: \xt Acts 9:26-30.\x*
\v 19 I didn’t meet any of the other ambassadors, other than Yeshua’s brother, Ya’akov \add (James; Grk. Yakobos)\add*,
\v 20 and what I’m writing to you, I’m writing with \nd God\nd* watching, so I’m not lying.
\p
\v 21 Then I went into the regions of Aram \add (Syria)\add* and Kilikia.
\v 22 The assemblies back in Yehudah \add (Judea)\add* that believe in \nd the messiah\nd* didn’t know me by sight,
\v 23 but had only heard about me—that the person who’d once been persecuting \add them\add* was now preaching the good message about the faith himself,
\v 24 and they praised \nd God\nd* because of \add what had happened to\add* me.
\c 2
\s1 The ambassadors accept Paulos and Barnabas
\p
\v 1 It was fourteen years later that I made the uphill trek to Yerushalem \add (Jerusalem)\add*\x + \xo 2:1: \xt Acts 11:30; 15:2.\x* with Barnabas, taking Titos along as well.
\v 2 \add God\add* had told me to go there, and I explained to the \add leaders there\add* the good message that I’d been proclaiming to the non-Jews. I did it privately, to double-check that I wasn’t somehow just acting vainly.
\v 3 But not even Titos, who was with me, was compelled to be circumcised, even though he’s a Greek.
\v 4 However, some false brothers were sneaked in to monitor the \add freedoms\add* that we have in \nd the messiah\nd*, \nd Yeshua\nd* \add (Jesus; Grk. Yesus)\add*, and they wanted us to be enslaved to their traditions.
\v 5 But we didn’t \add submit\add* to them even for one hour, so that the truth of the good message could continue amongst you.
\p
\v 6 And those people who were supposed \add leaders\add* (although what they were didn’t mean much to me, because \nd God\nd* doesn’t go by \add appearances\add*)\x + \xo 2:6: \xt Deu 10:17.\x* didn’t \add really\add* contribute anything useful, as far as I could tell.
\v 7 Instead, they saw that I’ve been entrusted with taking the good message to the non-Jews, just like Kefa \add (Peter; Grk. Petros)\add* \add was entrusted to take it\add* to the Yehudim \add (Jews)\add*,
\v 8 because the same \add God\add* who worked in Kefa to assign him to the Yehudim also worked in me \add to assign me\add* to the non-Jews.
\v 9 \add So\add*, seeing the grace that \add God\add* had shown towards me, Ya’akov \add (James; Grk. Yakobos)\add* and Kefa and Yohan \add (John; Grk. Yoannes)\add*, the apparent pillars \add of the assemblies\add*, accepted me and Barnabas as equals, so that we could go to the non-Jews and them to the Yehudim.
\v 10 Also, \add they said that\add* we need to keep helping the poor, but I was eager to do that anyway.
\s1 Paulos scolds Kefa
\p
\v 11 But when Kefa \add (Peter; Grk. Petros)\add* came to Antiokeia, I had to oppose him to his face, because he’d messed up.
\v 12 He’d been eating with non-Jews, but when Ya’akov \add (Jacob)\add* and his companions arrived, he about-faced and started eating separately, because he was afraid of \add being criticised by\add* the strict Yehudim \add (Jews)\add*.
\v 13 The other Yehudim with him were also being hypocrites—so much so that even Barnabas joined their hypocrisy—
\v 14 but when I saw that they weren’t being honest with the truth of the good message, I had to say to Kefa \add in front of\add* everybody, “If you Yehudim are living more like non-Jews, and not like \add strict\add* Yehudim, how can you force non-Jews to obey all the Jewish traditions?”
\s1 You can’t be saved by obeying rules
\p
\v 15 We follow Jewish laws, not other nations’ customs,
\v 16 but we know that we’re not saved by following rules,\x + \xo 2:16: \xt Psa 143:2; Rom 3:20.\x* but only by faith \nd in Yeshua \+add (Jesus; Grk. Yesus)\+add* the messiah\nd*.\x + \xo 2:16: \xt Rom 3:22.\x* So we’ve believed in \nd Yeshua the messiah\nd* so that we can be made guiltless by our faith \nd in the messiah\nd*, and not by obeying all those rules, because none of those rules can ever make any person guiltless.
\v 17 However, if we attempt to be made guiltless through \nd the messiah\nd*, yet find that we’re sinners, does that mean that \nd the messiah\nd* is a servant to sin? That could never be so!
\v 18 If I rebuild what I’ve already torn down, that would show me to be a law-breaker.
\v 19 It was the law that showed me that I needed die to the idea of following the law, so that I could live \add to serve\add* \nd God\nd* \add instead\add*. I was executed on a post along with \nd the messiah\nd*,\x + \xo 2:19: \xt Rom 6:1-11; 7:1-6.\x*
\v 20 and I’m not living any more, but it’s \nd the messiah\nd* now living in me. The way that \add my\add* body stays alive now is by living by faith in \nd God’s\nd* son—the \nd one\nd* who loved me and allowed himself to be executed for me.
\v 21 I’m not rejecting \nd God’s\nd* grace, because if the law could make me guiltless, that would mean that \nd the messiah\nd* died unnecessarily.
\c 3
\p
\v 1 You foolish Galatians, who tricked you into changing? You know that \nd Yeshua the messiah\nd* was publicly known to have been executed \add by being nailed to\add* a post.\x + \xo 3:1: \xt Jhn 20:25.\x*
\v 2 So I just want to ask you one thing: did you receive the \nd spirit\nd* by obeying all the rules, or was it by faith when you heard?
\v 3 So \add aren’t\add* you being foolish? Having begun \nd in the spirit\nd*, now you \add people\add* are focusing back on bodily discipline.
\v 4 You vainly endured so many \add things\add*—it was vain \add wasn’t\add* it?
\v 5 So the one who gave the \nd spirit\nd* to you and worked miracles among you, did all that come about from obeying the rules or by the faith that came from hearing \add the good message\add*?
\s1 The spreading of Avraham’s blessing
\p
\v 6 Avraham believed \nd in God\nd* and was declared guiltless as a result.\x + \xo 3:6: \xt Gen 15:6; Rom 4:3.\x*
\v 7 So now anyone who exercises faith is a descendant of Avraham.\x + \xo 3:7: \xt Rom 4:16.\x*
\v 8 The \add writers of the\add* \add scriptures\add* foresaw that \nd God\nd* was going to make non-Jews guiltless through faith when he promised to Avraham that all the nations would be blessed through him,\x + \xo 3:8: \xt Gen 12:3.\x*
\v 9 so those who have faith \add that Yeshua\add* \add (Jesus; Grk. Yesus)\add* \add is the messiah\add* will be blessed just as Avraham was blessed for believing \add God\add*.
\p
\v 10 All those who strive to be saved by obeying the law are under a curse, because it’s written that ‘everyone is cursed who doesn’t live by and fulfil everything that’s been written down in the law scroll’.\x + \xo 3:10: \xt Deu 27:26 (LXX).\x*
\v 11 It’s clear that the law can’t make anyone guiltless \add in \+nd God’s\+nd* view\add*, because \add it’s written that\add* ‘those who are guiltless will live by faith’.\x + \xo 3:11: \xt Hab 2:4.\x*
\v 12 And the law doesn’t produce faith, but ‘the person who obeys these things is the one who will live’.\x + \xo 3:12: \xt Lev 18:5.\x*
\p
\v 13 \nd The messiah\nd* saved us from the law’s curse by becoming the curse for our sake, because it’s written, ‘\nd anyone\nd* who’s hung on a tree is cursed’.\x + \xo 3:13: \xt Deu 21:23.\x*
\v 14 He \add paid the price for us\add* so that the blessing for Avraham would \add also\add* reach the non-Jews through \nd the messiah Yeshua\nd* and we’d \add all\add* be able to receive the \nd spirit\nd* through faith.
\s1 The law didn’t annul the promise
\p
\v 15 \add Brothers and sisters\add*, consider our human ways: once an agreement has been accepted, one party can’t just reject it or add conditions.
\v 16 The promises were given to Avraham and to his offspring. It doesn’t say ‘offsprings (plural)’ but is only referring to the \nd one\nd* who’s the \nd messiah\nd*.
\v 17 \add So\add* what I’m telling you is that the agreement \add with Avraham\add* that was previously ratified by \nd God\nd* was not annulled 430 years later\x + \xo 3:17: \xt Exo 12:40.\x* when the law was given \add to Mosheh\add* \add (Moses)\add*, and nor was the promise nullified,
\v 18 because if the inheritance \add now\add* came by obeying the law, then it could no longer be the result of the promise, but \nd God\nd* had already granted it to Avraham via the promise.\x + \xo 3:18: \xt Rom 4:14.\x*
\v 19 So what was the law for then? Because of sinfulness, it was added until the promised descendant would come. It was directed through messengers and entrusted to \add Mosheh as\add* a mediator.
\v 20 You don’t need a mediator if there’s only one party involved, and \nd God\nd* is one.
\s1 Coming of age for the inheritance
\p
\v 21 So does the law oppose \nd God’s\nd* promises? Certainly not, because if the law was able to give life, you could become guiltless by obeying the law.
\v 22 The \add scriptures\add* \add explain that\add* everyone is imprisoned by sin so that by faith \nd in Yeshua \+add (Jesus; Grk. Yesus)\+add* the messiah\nd*, the promise could be granted to those who believe.
\p
\v 23 Before faith could come, we were guarded under the law—imprisoned so that faith could be revealed.
\v 24 So the law was our tutor until \nd the messiah\nd* came so that we could then be made guiltless by faith,
\v 25 and now that we’ve come to faith, we’re no longer under \add our\add* tutor.
\p
\v 26 So you’re all \nd God’s\nd* children through faith in \nd the messiah\nd*, \nd Yeshua\nd*,
\v 27 because everyone who’s been immersed \add to obey\add* \nd the messiah\nd* is now cloaked in \nd the messiah\nd*.
\v 28 So there’s no Yehudi \add (Jew)\add* or Greek, no slave or free \add person\add*, no male or female, because you’re all \add identified\add* as being in \nd the messiah\nd*, \nd Yeshua\nd*.
\v 29 And if you’re in \nd the messiah\nd*, then you’re \add now\add* Avraham’s descendant, and so you’re heirs of that promise.\x + \xo 3:29: \xt Rom 4:13.\x*
\c 4
\p
\v 1 But as long as the heir is underage, he’s no wealthier than a slave (even though he’ll \add become\add* the master),
\v 2 but he’s under guardians and managers until he reaches the age that his father specified.
\v 3 That’s how it is with us—when we were infants we were enslaved by this world’s principles,
\v 4 but at the appointed time, \nd God\nd* sent his son down to be given birth to by a woman and to live under \add Mosheh’s\add* \add (Moses)\add* law
\v 5 so that he could \add buy back\add* those enslaved under the law and they \add could now be adopted\add* as children \add with an inheritance\add*.\x + \xo 4:5-7: \xt Rom 8:15-17.\x*
\p
\v 6 \add So\add* because you’re \add now\add* children, \nd God\nd* sent his son’s \nd spirit\nd* down into our hearts as we call out, ‘Father, father.’
\v 7 Now you’re no longer \add slaves\add*, but children \add with full rights\add*, and as children, \add heirs\add* of \nd God’s\nd* \add promise\add*.
\s1 Paulos’s concerns about their faith
\p
\v 8 When you didn’t know \nd God\nd*, you served others who aren’t really gods at all.
\v 9 But now that you do know \nd God\nd*, or rather now that \nd God\nd* knows you, why would you turn back again to useless and inferior beliefs, to serve them all over again?
\v 10 You \add people\add* observe \add special\add* holidays and \add certain\add* months and seasons and years.
\v 11 I worry that maybe I’ve wasted my time helping you.
\p
\v 12 I implore you to become like me, \add brothers and sisters\add*, because I’m also just like you. You haven’t wronged me in any way,
\v 13 but you know that it was a result of my fleshly weakness that I originally preached the good message to you.\f + \fr 4:13 \ft Scholars are unclear whether this might mean something like ill health, poor eyesight (see v15), or even a moral weakness, so we’ve left it as ambiguous in the translation as it is in the original.\f*
\v 14 Yet you didn’t mock or despise me, \add brothers and sisters,\add* for this trial of my flesh, but rather you received me as \nd God’s\nd* messenger like you might have received \nd the messiah Yeshua\nd* \add (Jesus; Grk. Yesus)\add* himself.
\v 15 So where are your \add generous attitudes\add* \add now\add*? I know that back then, you would have almost dug your own eyes out to give them to me.
\v 16 Have I \add now\add* become your enemy just because I told you \add people\add* the truth?
\p
\v 17 Those \add others\add* have worked hard to \add try to win\add* you \add over\add*, but not for good \add reasons\add*—they want to cut you off so that you’ll be enthusiastic about them.
\v 18 It’s good to be enthusiastic about \add things\add* that are always good, and not only at the times when I’m with you, \add brothers and sisters\add*.
\v 19 My dear ones, who I’m again labouring with in childbirth until \nd the messiah\nd* becomes part of you,
\v 20 I wanted to be there with you now, and to speak quite differently than this, because I’m puzzled about you.
\s1 The free line and the slaves
\p
\v 21 Are you \add people\add* who are under the law telling me that you’re not even hearing what the law is?
\v 22 It’s written \add in the scriptures\add* that Avraham had two sons: one \add son\add* with the slave girl \add Hagar\add*,\x + \xo 4:22: \xt Gen 16:15.\x* and one with the free woman.\x + \xo 4:22: \xt Gen 21:2.\x*
\v 23 The slave girl’s son was born because of human actions, but in contrast, the free woman’s son was a result of \add God’s\add* promise.
\v 24 So if we use this as an illustration, there are two agreements: one is from Mt. Sinay which relates to Hagar and gives birth to enslaved people.
\v 25 Hagar’s Mt. Sinay is in Arav and corresponds to the present \add earthly\add* Yerushalem \add (Jerusalem)\add* in slavery along with her children.
\v 26 However, the heavenly Yerushalem, which is our mother, is free,
\v 27 because it’s been written \add in the scriptures\add*:
\q1 ‘Be glad barren woman,
\q2 you who have never borne a child.
\q1 Be bold and shout,
\q2 you who have never been in labour,
\q1 because the lonely woman \add now\add* has many children,
\q2 even more than the woman with a husband.’\x + \xo 4:27: \xt Isa 54:1.\x*
\p
\v 28 And you, \add brothers and sisters\add*, are children of the promise, like Yitshak \add (Isaac)\add*,
\v 29 but back then the one born because of human actions persecuted the one \add born\add* because of the \nd spirit\nd*,\x + \xo 4:29: \xt Gen 21:9.\x* and that’s how it continues now.
\v 30 But what do the \add scriptures\add* say? ‘Send away the slave girl and her son, because the slave girl’s son won’t be getting the \add inheritance\add* alongside the free woman’s son.’\x + \xo 4:30: \xt Gen 21:10.\x*
\v 31 So, \add brothers and sisters\add*, we’re not \add descendants\add* of the slave girl, but of the free woman.
\c 5
\s1 Don’t let the law destroy your faith
\p
\v 1 The \nd messiah\nd* has freed us so we can have freedom, so stand firm and don’t get entangled again in the burden of slavery.
\p
\v 2 What’s more, I, Paulos would add that if you \add men\add* got circumcised, then \nd the messiah\nd* wouldn’t be of any benefit.
\v 3 I’ll say it again \add clearly\add*: every man who \add gets himself\add* circumcised has put himself in debt to obeying all of the law.
\v 4 You were released from \nd the messiah\nd* when you moved to trusting in the law in order to be right with God—you fell away from grace at that point.
\v 5 We who are in \nd the spirit\nd* are eagerly waiting for the righteousness that we’re expecting to receive by faith,
\v 6 because in \nd the messiah Yeshua\nd* \add (Jesus; Grk. Yesus)\add*, it’s not circumcision that achieves anything, nor uncircumcision, but faith worked out through love.
\p
\v 7 You \add people\add* were running the race so well—who held you back from believing the truth?
\v 8 It wasn’t the one who called you.
\v 9 A little bit of yeast can make a whole loaf of bread rise.\x + \xo 5:9: \xt 1Cor 5:6.\x*
\v 10 I’m trusting in \nd Yahweh\nd* that you won’t be thinking anything different \add from the truth\add*. The one who’ll bear the judgement is the one who’s been confusing you, whoever that was.
\p
\v 11 But, \add brothers and sisters\add*, if I was still preaching the Jewish rules, why would I still be being persecuted? If I was still preaching that, then talking about \add dying on a post\add* wouldn’t even be offensive.
\v 12 I wish that the ones upsetting you \add people by suggesting cutting off bits\add* would cut it all off.
\p
\v 13 \add Brothers and sisters\add*, you were called into freedom. That doesn’t mean that you should use the freedom to do whatever your minds desire, but rather, you should be serving each other in love,
\v 14 because the entire law can be fulfilled by obeying one rule: Love your neighbour like yourself.\x + \xo 5:14: \xt Lev 19:18.\x*
\v 15 But if you’re snapping at and bullying each other, watch out, or else you’ll destroy each other’s \add faith\add*.
\s1 Being led by God’s spirit
\p
\v 16 I’ll tell you: allow yourselves to be led \nd by the spirit\nd*, and then your earthly \add desires\add* won’t triumph,
\v 17 because our earthly desires oppose the \nd spirit\nd*, and \nd the spirit\nd* \add opposes\add* our earthly desires—they oppose each other, so that’s why you end up doing things that you didn’t \add really\add* want to do.\x + \xo 5:17: \xt Rom 7:15-23.\x*
\v 18 But if you’re being led \nd by the spirit\nd* then you’re not \add trying to earn your way to God by\add* obeying all of Mosheh’s \add (Moses)\add* rules.
\p
\v 19 The actions that come from our earthly desires include: sexual immorality, impurity, sensuality,
\v 20 idolatry, sorcery, hostility, strife, jealousies, rages, selfish ambitions, arguing, divisions,
\v 21 \add envy\add*, \add drunkenness\add*, \add carousing\add*, and other similar \add things\add* which I’ve mentioned previously. And as I \add also\add* said previously, the people that do these things won’t be inheriting \nd God’s\nd* kingdom.
\p
\v 22 But the end result of having \add God’s\add* \nd spirit\nd* is \add to show\add* love, joy, peace, patience, kindness, goodness, faith,
\v 23 gentleness, and self-control—none \add of\add* \add Mosheh’s\add* \add laws\add* are against any of these!
\v 24 And for \nd the messiah Yeshua’s\nd* \add (Jesus; Grk. Yesus)\add* \add apprentices\add*, earthly desires need to be executed on a post, along with their passions and lusts.
\v 25 If we’re \add being led\add* \nd by\nd* \add God’s\add* \nd spirit\nd* in our living, then we should also be aligning \add our values\add* \nd by the spirit\nd*.
\v 26 We have to take care not to become conceited, not to provoke each other, and not to envy each other.
\c 6
\s1 Helping each other
\p
\v 1 \add Brothers and sisters\add*, if a person has been caught sinning, you spiritual \add people\add* need to guide the person with a spirit of gentleness, at the same time looking at your own behaviour, in case you yourselves are also tempted.
\v 2 Help those who are going through difficulties or pain, and in doing so you’ll fulfil \nd the messiah’s\nd* law,
\v 3 because anyone who supposes themself to be someone special, but isn’t, is only deceiving themself.
\v 4 Evaluate \add your\add* own actions, but keep \add your\add* \add boasts\add* to \add yourself\add* and don’t tell others,
\v 5 because each person must face up to their own issues.
\p
\v 6 \add Those\add* who are being taught should share all their blessings with \add those\add* who are teaching.
\p
\v 7 Don’t be fooled—you can’t \add get away with\add* mocking \nd God\nd*, because what you sow determines what you’ll harvest.
\v 8 Anyone who sows \add seed\add* to feed their earthly desires will harvest earthly corruption, but anyone who sows \nd spiritual\nd* \add seeds\add* will harvest eternal life from the \nd spirit\nd*.
\v 9 Don’t be discouraged from doing good \add things\add*, because we’ll have a \add good\add* harvest in due course if we don’t give up.
\v 10 So then, as time allows it, we should be working towards good for everybody, and especially for fellow believers.
\s1 Paulos’s final advice
\p
\v 11 \sig Look how big these letters are when it’s my own handwriting.\sig*
\v 12 \sig There’s so many people that want to look good in earthly terms, and they’re the ones trying to persuade you that you have to fulfil all of Mosheh’s \+add (Moses)\+add* rules—they don’t want to be persecuted concerning \+nd the\+nd* \+nd messiah\+nd* \+add dying on a post\+add*.\sig*
\v 13 \sig Even those who are circumcised don’t obey all the rules, but they’d still like for your men to be circumcised so that they can claim them as followers.\sig*
\v 14 \sig As for me, may I never boast, except about our \+nd master Yeshua\+nd* \+add (Jesus; Grk. Yesus)\+add* \+nd the\+nd* \+nd messiah\+nd*, \+add whose life was cut off on the\+add* post. Through him, it’s like the entire world has been cut off from me, and I’ve been cut off from the world,\sig*
\v 15 \sig because obeying Mosheh’s law is nothing, and not needing to obey it is nothing, but being a new creation \+add is everything\+add*.\sig*
\v 16 \sig And everyone who follows that principle, may peace and mercy be on them and on \nd God’s\nd* Yisrael \+add (Israel)\+add*.\sig*
\p
\v 17 \sig As for the other troubles, don’t bother me with them, because I carry the marks on my body of \+add someone who follows\+add* \+nd Yeshua\+nd*.\sig*
\p
\v 18 \sig May the grace from our \+nd master\+nd* \+nd Yeshua\+nd* \+nd the\+nd* \+nd messiah\+nd* be with your spirit, \+add brothers and sisters\+add*. That’s how I want it to be.\sig*
